{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for exact clean pushed head ec453b920460b2961a964c6b5d3921ca27e72d17 and primary cmd/gs artifact cd97fd0687e5275900ef52d8057e5267e96a4923.\n\nThe strict implementer check closes the concrete wrong-signer defect. TestMergeRefusesASignerWhoDidNotDoTheApprovedWork runs the original shape: the independent reviewer calls merge on the operator implementation. The refusal happens before the receipt ref is reserved, leaves target HEAD unchanged, leaves projection depth unchanged, and then the implementer can use the same unspent approval successfully.\n\nThe ratifier escape clause is a blocking authority and failure-atomicity defect. requireApprovedImplementer admits a ratifier from one pre-Git projection snapshot. Ratifier standing is mutable. Another authorized actor can retire that standing while the tentative Git merge runs, or while the post-commit succession batch appends act by act. recordMergeSuccession checks the standing again only after HEAD has committed; the fold checks it again at each supersession. A revoked ratifier therefore reaches the same forbidden outcome this repair is meant to remove: Git has moved, then succession is refused and stranded. The strict artifact-author fingerprint is immutable and does not have this race.\n\nThe exception also conflicts with the stated Workroom contract. docs/reference/architecture.md says the merge receipt lets the implementer of an approved head retire cross-author predecessors. validateMergeReceiptNow enforces receipt.record.Actor == artifact.record.Actor. decideSupersede gives a live ratifier separate free-standing authority; it does not make a ratifier the approved implementer or give the receipt that authority. docs/reference/gs/merge.md now claims the exception, but the architecture contract was neither changed consistently nor updated in this commit.\n\nRequired repair: remove the ratifier exception from requireApprovedImplementer and the merge page, so --as must resolve